I wrote a piece for the wall sorry for  the Washington Post that came out the  morning after the election on Wednesday  the fifth where I explained what I  thought we needed to do as Republicans  to find our way out of the wilderness  and I suggested that we return to the  first principles of the Republican Party  limited government economic freedom  those are the principles those have been  always been the first and second article  of faith for Republicans and that's what  can propel us back into the majority  because that I think people will come to  realize particularly where we're going  with the bailout and where the economy  is going that that we simply cannot dig  our way out of this by replacing  economic incentives with political  incentives I mentioned I wrote that  while Adam Smith's Wealth of Nations may  be on the discount rack this year the  free market is still the most efficient  way to allocate resources and human  allocate capital and human resources in  an economy and Americans know it now I I  think that that certainly is the case  I think Republicans also need to realize  that focusing on the wedge issues of the  past will not grow the Republican Party  the tone and tenor of the immigration  debate has done the Republican Party  immeasurable damage and focusing on  social issues while ignoring our  principles on limited government and  free markets will not broaden our base  but I am optimistic I really am  when you're at rock bottom there's only  one way to go but not just for that but  but I think that we as I mentioned our  strongest suit as and I'm speaking as a  Republican here and I know that  everybody here is not but our strongest  suit is limited government economic  freedom individual liberty and if we'll  get back to those first principles with  groups like reason to be there as the  lodestar for us then we can certainly  gain favor again and also educate those  who need to be educated and and much as  reason has done for 40 years I've been  so pleased to have an association with  reason for a long time I headed the  Goldwater Institute for seven years and  I always wanted to be like The Reason  Foundation and dealing with Bob Poole  and Bill Eggers and others on  transportation and other issues so I've  admired reason for a long time long  before I got to Congress but I wish that  more of my colleagues would subscribe to  reason and also subscribe to the  principles that you've enunciated so  well so thank you so much for having me  here it's an honor to be here at this  anniversary  you
 

